        a: `The first call I make is not to engineering. It is to our legal and compliance team because the 90 day clock and the actual scope of what needs to change are two different things and I have seen companies panic build for regulatory deadlines only to discover they misread the circular.\n\nSo day one is about getting absolute clarity on three things. Which specific flows in our UPI integration are affected? Is this a back end change, a user flow change, or both? And is there any grace period or phased compliance pathway that the RBI has indicated?\n\nOnce I have that clarity, usually within 24 to 48 hours, I do the math on engineering effort. In my experience, RBI guideline changes to UPI integrations typically fall into one of two buckets. Either they are configuration and API changes that take two to three weeks of focused engineering time, or they are architectural changes that could take six to eight weeks. The answer to that question determines everything about how I manage the roadmap conversation.\n\nWith the enterprise clients I reach out proactively before they hear about the regulatory change from anywhere else. I frame the conversation as us protecting their compliance posture, not us deprioritizing their commitments. Most enterprise fintech clients understand regulatory timelines because they live with them too. What they do not forgive is surprises.\n\nInternally I go to the roadmap conversation with a clear trade off document. Here is what we committed to, here is what compliance requires, here is the engineering bandwidth, and here are three options for how we sequence the next 90 days. I never walk into a locked roadmap conversation asking leadership to make a choice I should have already made. I walk in with a recommendation and the data to back it.`,
