        q: "Walk me through how you would define the product requirements for a real time fraud detection system for a UPI payments platform processing 10 million transactions a day.",
        a: `Before I get into requirements I want to anchor on what real time actually means in this context because that word gets used loosely and the answer changes everything about the architecture we end up specifying.\n\nFor a UPI platform at 10 million transactions a day, real time to the regulator means the transaction decision happens before settlement, which gives you roughly 30 seconds. Real time to the user means they do not notice a delay, which gives you under 500 milliseconds. Real time to the fraud team means they can intervene before money moves, which is somewhere in between. Aligning on which definition we are building for is the first product decision I make.\n\nI will assume we are targeting under 500 milliseconds for the detection decision because at this transaction volume, anything slower starts to degrade user experience at scale and creates customer service pressure.\n\nFor requirements I think across four dimensions. Detection accuracy, which means I need to define acceptable false positive and false negative rates. A false positive in UPI payments means a legitimate transaction gets blocked and an angry customer calls support. A false negative means fraud goes through and we absorb the loss. The right balance depends on our merchant mix and our risk appetite, but I would typically target a false positive rate under 0.5% and a false negative rate under 0.1% as starting benchmarks.\n\nLatency and throughput requirements come next. At 10 million transactions a day that is roughly 115 transactions per second on average with peaks likely hitting 3 to 4 times that during festive seasons or salary credit days in India. The system has to hold those latency targets under peak load, not average load.\n\nThe third dimension is the feature set that feeds the model. Transaction amount and velocity, device fingerprint, geolocation delta, beneficiary account age, time of day patterns, and UPI VPA behavior are the signals I would prioritize in the first version. I would not try to build everything at once because model complexity has diminishing returns early and increases the risk of false positives.\n\nThe fourth dimension is the intervention workflow. Detection without intervention design is incomplete. When the model flags a transaction I need to define what happens. Do we block and ask for step up authentication? Do we allow but flag for review? Do we block outright above certain thresholds? Each of those paths has a different user experience and a different regulatory implication under the RBI fraud framework.\n\nThe requirement I would push hardest for with engineering is explainability. At this transaction volume we will have customers disputing blocks daily and the fraud team needs to be able to pull a reason code for every flagged transaction in under 30 seconds. A black box model that cannot explain its decisions creates operational debt that compounds faster than people expect.`,
