        q: "You have just launched a buy now pay later product for a fintech targeting salaried professionals in Tier 2 Indian cities. What metrics do you track in the first 90 days and what would make you pull the product back?",
        a: `The first 90 days for a BNPL product in this segment are about understanding whether your credit model works in the real world, not whether your marketing worked. I have seen teams celebrate activation numbers in month one only to face a default spike in month three that the original model never anticipated.\n\nSo I track metrics across three layers.\n\nThe first layer is credit performance. Weekly cohort default rates by days past due, specifically DPD 30, DPD 60, and DPD 90. I compare these against the model predictions at time of approval. If the actual DPD 30 rate for the first cohort is running more than 1.5 times the predicted rate, that is a signal I take seriously immediately. I also watch the distribution of approved credit scores versus actual repayment behavior to understand where the model is weakest.\n\nThe second layer is unit economics. Revenue per disbursed loan, cost of collections per delinquent account, processing cost per application, and the net interest margin after provisions. For a Tier 2 city salaried professional product I would expect collections costs to be higher than urban cohorts because the physical collections infrastructure is thinner. If my cost of collections is eating more than 40% of my interest income in month two, the product math does not work.\n\nThe third layer is customer behavior. Repayment channel mix, because customers who repay via auto debit have significantly lower default rates than those who make manual payments. Second loan uptake rate within 90 days, because a healthy BNPL product should see 30 to 40% of good borrowers taking a second loan. And support ticket volume by reason, because a spike in payment confusion tickets usually predicts a collections problem four to six weeks out.\n\nThe conditions under which I would pull the product back are specific. If my DPD 30 rate in week eight is more than twice the model prediction, I pause new disbursals and audit the underwriting logic before issuing another loan. If my net interest margin after provisions goes negative in month two, the product is destroying value faster than I can optimize it. And if I see any pattern suggesting the product is being used for purposes outside the intended use case, I stop and investigate before scaling.\n\nPulling a BNPL product is not a failure. Continuing to disburse when the early signals are negative is.`,
