        q: "You are a senior PM at a neobank and your data team tells you that a feature you championed and shipped three months ago has had zero measurable impact on the metric it was supposed to move. Your CPO asks you to present findings to the leadership team. How do you handle this?",
        a: `I present it honestly and I do not bury the result in methodology caveats.\n\nThe temptation in this situation is to spend the first 10 minutes of the presentation explaining why the metric did not move in ways that are technically true but functionally designed to protect the feature. The model had confounding variables. The cohort was too small. The measurement window was too short. I have sat in enough of those presentations to know that senior leadership sees through it and loses trust in the PM faster than if they had just said the feature did not work.\n\nSo my presentation structure is direct. Here is what we shipped, here is what we expected to see, here is what actually happened, here is what I think explains the gap, and here is what I recommend we do next.\n\nOn the explanation, I go in having already done the diagnostic work. Did the feature reach the users it was designed for? If adoption was low, the feature did not fail, distribution did. Did the feature work as designed technically? If there were implementation gaps, the product hypothesis may still be valid. Did the underlying user problem actually exist at the scale we assumed? If not, we built the right solution for the wrong problem size.\n\nIn a neobank context specifically, I would also look at whether macroeconomic factors during the three months affected the metric independently of the feature. Interest rate changes, RBI policy shifts, or competitor moves can suppress or inflate metrics in ways that make feature attribution genuinely difficult.\n\nWhat I never do is recommend we give the feature more time without a specific hypothesis for why the next 90 days would be different from the last 90 days. More time without a changed variable is not a strategy.\n\nMy recommendation to the CPO is usually one of three things. We sunset the feature and document the learning. We run a targeted experiment with a modified version of the feature based on what the data is telling us. Or we reframe the metric because the feature may be generating value that we are not measuring correctly. But I come with a recommendation, not a question.`,
