        q: "You are a PM at a private bank and your team has just discovered that a bug in the loan EMI calculation module has been overcharging approximately 8,000 customers by small amounts for the past six months. The total overcharge across all affected customers is around 45 lakh rupees. What do you do?",
        a: `The first call I make is to our Chief Compliance Officer and General Counsel simultaneously. This is not a product decision. It is a regulatory and legal event and I need to understand our disclosure obligations before I make any other move.\n\nIn Indian banking, systematic overcharging of customers for six months is likely a reportable event under RBI guidelines. The question is not whether we remediate. We absolutely remediate. The question is the sequence and the communication.\n\nWhile legal is assessing disclosure obligations I do three things in parallel. I ask engineering to confirm the exact affected population with precise numbers. 8,000 is an estimate and I need the exact count, the exact overcharge per customer, and the exact date range before we communicate anything. Corrections made on imprecise data create a second remediation problem. I ask finance to confirm the total liability figure and validate the 45 lakh number. And I ask engineering for a root cause analysis on the EMI calculation bug so I understand whether this is isolated or whether it could affect other calculation modules.\n\nOnce I have legal guidance on disclosure obligations I work with communications to design the customer notification. In my view customers should be notified before the credit hits their account, not after. A customer who sees an unexpected credit in their account without explanation calls support, which creates operational load and erodes trust. A customer who receives a clear explanation of what happened and what we are doing about it before the credit lands is more likely to remain loyal despite the error.\n\nThe remediation itself needs to include not just the principal overcharge but also any interest those funds would have earned had they remained with the customer. That is the standard in banking remediation and falling short of it creates regulatory risk even if leadership pushes back on the cost.\n\nI own the product bug. I present it to leadership with complete information, a clear remediation plan, and a root cause analysis that explains what process changes we are implementing to prevent recurrence. What I never do is minimize the scope or delay the remediation to manage the optics.`,
