        q: "You are a PM at a telecom and TRAI mandates that you must provide customers with a simple mechanism to port out to a competitor within 24 hours. Your current MNP process takes five to seven days. How do you build the product that meets this mandate while managing internal commercial concerns?",
        a: `The first thing I establish internally is that this is a compliance requirement, not a commercial choice, and the product I build has to genuinely meet the mandate, not find ways to technically comply while making the process difficult in practice.\n\nI say that explicitly because the history of mobile number portability implementations globally is full of operators who built technically compliant processes that were operationally designed to discourage porting through friction, complexity, or delay. TRAI has specifically mandated 24 hours because the previous process was too slow and a 24 hour implementation that introduces new friction to compensate is going to attract regulatory attention.\n\nOn the technical side, the 24 hour port out requirement requires significant work on our provisioning and interoperability processes. The current five to seven day timeline exists partly because of manual steps in the verification and provisioning workflow and partly because of coordination timelines with the number portability clearinghouse. I work with our network and operations teams to understand which steps in the current process are genuinely unavoidable due to technical dependencies and which steps are administrative delays that can be eliminated.\n\nFor the customer facing experience, I design the port out initiation as a self serve flow in our app and USSD menu that requires the minimum information legally necessary. I do not add verification steps that go beyond the regulatory requirement.\n\nFor the internal commercial concerns, I acknowledge them honestly. Port out acceleration will likely increase our churn rate in the short term because some customers who intended to port out eventually gave up because the process was too slow. That is the commercial cost of compliance and it is not a cost we can avoid by designing a bad product.\n\nThe commercial response to the mandate is retention investment, not product friction. I work with the commercial team to design proactive retention offers that trigger when a customer initiates a port out request. A customer who sees a compelling offer at the point of port out initiation is more likely to reconsider than a customer who encounters a slow or confusing process and becomes more determined to leave.`,
