        q: "You are a PM at a bank and your engineering team discovers a security vulnerability in a feature that has been live for two years. The vulnerability has not been exploited but has the potential for significant customer data exposure. How do you handle this?",
        a: `The first call I make is to the CISO and General Counsel simultaneously. A security vulnerability with potential for significant customer data exposure is not a product team decision. It is a legal, regulatory, and security decision and my role is to support it with complete information, not to lead it.\n\nIn the first two hours I work with the security and engineering teams to build a precise picture of the vulnerability. What is the attack vector? What customer data is potentially accessible? What would an attacker need to know or do to exploit it? Has there been any anomalous access to the affected systems that might indicate the vulnerability has been discovered by external parties even if not formally exploited? The CISO and legal team need these answers to assess our disclosure obligations accurately.\n\nWhile security is doing that assessment, engineering prepares a remediation plan with a specific timeline. I want to know whether the vulnerability can be patched in hours, days, or weeks, because the remediation timeline affects every other decision about disclosure and interim mitigations.\n\nIf the vulnerability is patchable quickly and there is no evidence of exploitation, the immediate priority is deploying the patch. Proactive disclosure to regulators, specifically RBI under their cybersecurity guidelines for banks, happens in parallel and the timing of customer notification is a legal decision based on the severity of the potential exposure and the regulatory requirements.\n\nIf the patch timeline is longer, interim mitigations become critical. Disabling the affected feature entirely may be the right call even at the cost of customer functionality, because the alternative is maintaining a known vulnerability in a live customer-facing system.\n\nThe two-year history of the vulnerability being present without exploitation is not a reason for complacency. It is information about the difficulty of discovering and exploiting it, which is relevant to the risk assessment but does not change our obligation to remediate and disclose according to our regulatory requirements.`,
