        q: "You are a PM at an insurance company and you need to sunset a product that has loyal but declining customers. How do you manage the transition ethically and commercially?",
        a: `Sunsetting an insurance product is categorically different from sunsetting a software feature because insurance customers have ongoing coverage that they depend on and in many cases cannot immediately replace without a gap in protection.\n\nThe ethical obligations in an insurance product sunset are specific. Customers need enough notice to find alternative coverage before their current coverage lapses. What constitutes enough notice varies by product type and customer vulnerability, but in most insurance contexts 90 days is a minimum and 180 days is more appropriate for customers who may have difficulty finding alternative coverage due to health conditions, age, or claims history.\n\nI design the sunset communication in phases. The first communication, at the start of the notice period, explains that the product will be discontinued, why, and what the timeline is. It includes a clear statement of what remains covered until the discontinuation date and what customers need to do to find alternative coverage. The second communication, at the midpoint, checks in on whether customers have found alternatives and offers assistance. The third communication, near the end of the notice period, provides final confirmation and clear information about what happens to any claims filed after discontinuation.\n\nFor customers who are most vulnerable, specifically those who may have difficulty finding alternative coverage due to pre-existing conditions or other factors, I work with the commercial team to explore whether a run-off arrangement with another insurer or a transfer of the policy book to a similar product can reduce the burden on those customers. A clean product sunset that leaves vulnerable customers uninsured is both an ethical failure and a regulatory risk.\n\nOn the commercial side, I manage the timeline to minimize new policies being issued while ensuring existing customers are served through to the sunset date. Stopping new policy issuance significantly before the sunset date, while honoring all existing policies through their terms, is the standard approach and it protects both customers and the company's regulatory standing.`,
