        q: "You are a PM at an insurtech and your user research shows that customers want lower premiums more than any other product improvement. Your actuarial team says lower premiums would make the product unprofitable. How do you address this tension?",
        a: `Customers wanting lower prices is not a product insight. It is a universal human preference that applies to every product in every market. The fact that research shows this does not tell you anything actionable about product strategy unless you understand why customers feel the current premium is not worth the value they receive.\n\nThe useful question underneath the premium preference is not how do we lower premiums but why do customers feel the current premium does not represent good value? Value perception in insurance has two components: the price paid and the benefit received. If customers feel the premium is too high, it is either because the premium is genuinely above market, or because customers do not perceive the benefit clearly enough to feel the premium is justified.\n\nA benefit perception problem is fixable without lowering premiums. Customers who understand clearly what they are covered for, who have experienced claims being settled promptly, and who see tangible non-claims value from their policy feel differently about their premium than customers who pay annually and receive nothing visible in return. Improving how we communicate coverage, improving claims experience, and adding non-claims value through wellness or prevention features are all interventions that improve value perception without reducing the premium.\n\nIf the research shows customers believe our premium is above market for comparable coverage, that is a pricing competitiveness issue and it requires the actuarial team to assess whether there are cost efficiencies that would allow competitive repricing without destroying margin. That analysis is different from simply lowering premiums to match customer wishes.\n\nI bring the actuarial team and the product team together with the research findings framed as a value perception problem rather than a price problem. The actuarial team can engage with the question of how to make the product more competitive without margin destruction. They cannot engage productively with the question of how to lower premiums to match customer preferences when those preferences would make the product unprofitable.`,
