        q: "You are a PM at an insurance company and your product has a feature that works perfectly technically but that users consistently misuse in ways that lead to poor outcomes for them. How do you address this?",
        a: `A feature that users consistently misuse is not a user education problem. It is a design problem. If the intended use of a feature is not the natural use, the design has failed to create the right mental model and the solution is redesign, not a help article.\n\nI start by documenting precisely what misuse looks like and what outcomes it produces for users. Misuse that leads to poor outcomes for users in an insurance context is particularly serious because the consequence is often that a customer believes they have coverage they do not have, or files a claim they believe is valid and is disappointed when it is not. That is not a UX problem. It is a customer welfare problem.\n\nThe design fix depends on the specific nature of the misuse. If users are misusing the feature because they misunderstand what it does, the fix is in the entry point and the feature description before the user commits to using it. Showing users the outcome before they start, rather than after they have already made a decision, prevents the misuse without friction.\n\nIf users are misusing the feature because the design makes the wrong path easier than the right path, the fix is in the interaction design itself. Making the correct use path more prominent, faster, and more rewarding than the incorrect path changes behavior more reliably than any amount of guidance content.\n\nIf the misuse is driven by a genuine gap between what users want to do and what the product was designed to support, the question is whether we should redesign the feature to support what users are actually trying to do. Consistent misuse in the same direction is often telling you that the intended design was wrong about what users need.`,
