        q: "You are a PM at a telecom and your product team has just completed a major accessibility audit that reveals your app is not usable by customers with visual impairments. How do you prioritize the remediation?",
        a: `An accessibility audit that reveals your app is not usable by visually impaired customers is not a nice-to-have improvement. It is a product that is excluding a segment of your customer base and in many jurisdictions a potential legal compliance issue under disability rights legislation.\n\nBefore I prioritize remediation I want to understand the scope precisely. What percentage of our customer base has visual impairments? What specific features are inaccessible? Are we talking about a few screens that need screen reader labels, or is the fundamental interaction design built in a way that is incompatible with assistive technology? The scope determines the timeline and the resource requirement.\n\nI triage the remediation into three categories. Critical barriers are features that visually impaired customers need to use the app at all, specifically login, account overview, and payment flows. These get fixed first regardless of engineering cost because a customer who cannot log in or pay their bill has no access to the product. Important gaps are features that impaired customers could work around but that create significant friction. These get fixed in the second sprint. Enhancement opportunities are features that work but could be improved for impaired users. These go in the roadmap but do not block other work.\n\nI also ask the legal team to assess our compliance obligations. In India, the Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act creates accessibility obligations for digital services and a telecom that ignores a known accessibility failure has regulatory exposure beyond the customer experience problem.\n\nFor the remediation process I involve users with visual impairments in testing the fixes. Screen reader compatibility testing done only by sighted engineers who are simulating impairment is significantly less reliable than testing done by actual users of assistive technology. I build that user testing into the remediation cycle, not as a final step but as a continuous check throughout.`,
