  q: "You are a project manager at a fintech company and a team member asks you to clarify the acceptance criteria for a user story because they are unclear. What do you do?",
  a: `Unclear acceptance criteria before development starts is the right time to catch this, not after the work is built and in testing.\n\nI bring the team member, the product owner, and if relevant the business analyst into a short focused session to walk through the user story together. The goal is to leave with acceptance criteria that are specific enough that the team member can write a test against each one. Criteria like the user should be able to view their transaction history is not testable. Criteria like the user can view the last 90 days of transactions with date, amount, merchant name, and status displayed is testable.\n\nI ask the product owner to describe what done looks like for this story in plain language, and I have the team member repeat back their understanding. The gap between what the product owner says and what the team member hears is usually where the ambiguity lives.\n\nI also check whether this is a one-off clarity issue or a pattern. If acceptance criteria are regularly unclear at the start of development, the problem is in the story writing process, not in individual stories, and that process needs to improve before the next sprint planning session.`,
