  q: "You are a project manager at a fintech. You are four weeks into a six-week project when the business sponsor tells you the project objective has changed completely due to a market shift. The new objective requires rebuilding half of what has been completed. How do you manage this?",
  a: `A complete change in project objective four weeks into a six-week project is not a scope change. It is effectively a new project using the same team, and I frame it that way explicitly with the business sponsor.\n\nThe first conversation I have is with the sponsor to understand specifically what has changed in the market and why the original objective is no longer viable. I need to understand the business rationale for the change before I can assess what response is appropriate. Sometimes what is described as a complete objective change is actually a significant but manageable scope change. Sometimes it genuinely is a new project.\n\nIf the objective has genuinely changed, I present the sponsor with an honest assessment of what can be salvaged from the four weeks of completed work. In most cases, some completed work transfers to the new objective — infrastructure, security architecture, integration frameworks — even when the product functionality is different. I want to accurately credit what is reusable before presenting the restart cost.\n\nI prepare a revised project plan for the new objective. This plan starts from today, not from the original week one. It has a new scope, a new timeline, and a new budget estimate. I present this to the steering committee as a formal project change that requires re-approval, because the original project that was approved no longer exists.\n\nI also have a direct conversation with the team. Four weeks of work being substantially rebuilt is demoralizing and the team deserves an honest explanation of why the change happened and what was beyond anyone's control. Teams that understand the context for pivots handle them more effectively than teams that are simply told the direction has changed.`,
