  q: "You are a project manager at an insurance company. Your project to implement IFRS 17 compliance has a hard regulatory deadline that is nine months away. You are four months in and your assessment shows you are unlikely to meet the deadline with current resources. What is your recovery strategy?",
  a: `An IFRS 17 compliance deadline is not negotiable. IFRS 17 is an international accounting standard with a mandatory adoption date set by the regulator and the IRDAI in India. Missing it is not a project management failure with a recovery plan — it is a regulatory violation with financial reporting consequences.\n\nFour months in with five months remaining and an assessment showing likely non-completion means I need to act on multiple fronts simultaneously and immediately.\n\nThe first action is a precise scope and gap analysis. What specifically is incomplete and how much effort is required to complete each remaining element? Not a high-level estimate — a bottom-up task-level assessment. IFRS 17 implementation has specific mandatory components: the measurement models, the discount rate methodology, the contractual service margin calculations, the presentation and disclosure requirements, and the systems and data changes to support them. I need to know the completion status of each.\n\nBased on that analysis I present the steering committee with a resource augmentation request. If the deadline is hard and the current team cannot meet it, the only options are to add resources, reduce scope to the minimum viable compliance, or accept non-compliance. Adding resources should be the first option explored because the other two have significant consequences.\n\nI also assess whether any elements of the IFRS 17 implementation can be fast-tracked without compromising their integrity. In my experience, technology and system changes typically take the longest to implement and test in IFRS 17 programs. If we are behind on the systems component, the question is whether manual processes or interim workarounds can support IFRS 17 compliant reporting while the permanent system solution is completed.\n\nI engage our external auditors in the conversation. Auditors who are aware of the implementation timeline and the constraints can provide guidance on what constitutes acceptable IFRS 17 compliance for the first reporting period, which sometimes gives more flexibility than the project team assumes.`,
