  q: "You are a project manager at a telecom company. Your project has a weekly team meeting but team members consistently come unprepared, making the meetings unproductive. What do you fix?",
  a: `Consistently unprepared team members in weekly meetings is a meeting design problem, not a team discipline problem. If preparation is not happening, either the expectation is not clear or the effort required is not proportionate to the benefit.\n\nI start by assessing what preparation the meeting actually requires. If team members are expected to arrive with status updates, risk assessments, and blockers ready to discuss, is that expectation communicated in advance with enough time to prepare? A meeting invitation sent the morning of the meeting with no agenda gives people no time to prepare even if they want to.\n\nI redesign the meeting with a clear agenda sent at least 24 hours in advance, specific preparation items assigned to each attendee, and a defined time limit. For each agenda item I specify what input I need from that person so preparation is concrete rather than open-ended.\n\nI also question whether the meeting itself is the right format. If the purpose is status updates, a shared written status update before the meeting and a 15-minute focused discussion of blockers and decisions may serve better than a full hour where everyone rehashes their work.\n\nA meeting that people come prepared for usually indicates it is genuinely valuable to them. A meeting they consistently skip preparation for often indicates it is not — and that is useful information about whether the meeting should continue in its current form.`,
