  q: "Halfway through your project, a new regulation comes into force that makes the product you are building technically legal but ethically questionable. The sponsor wants to continue. What do you do?",
  a: `A product that is technically legal but ethically questionable is not a project management problem that I can or should resolve on my own. It is a values and governance question that requires a much broader conversation.\n\nMy first step is to be precise about what I mean by ethically questionable. Vague ethical concerns are not actionable. Specific concerns are. If I believe the product we are building will cause identifiable harm to customers who cannot reasonably protect themselves, or that it will deceive customers in ways they would object to if fully informed, or that it will exploit a regulatory gap in a way that will attract future regulatory action, I can describe those concerns specifically.\n\nWith that specific articulation I raise my concerns with the project sponsor in a direct conversation. I tell them what I believe the ethical issue is and what I think the risk is for the organization. This includes regulatory risk — products that are technically legal today but that regulators have signaled concern about are often regulated out of existence within a product cycle, making the investment wasteful. It includes reputational risk — customers, employees, and investors make choices based on organizational ethics, not just legality. And it includes personal risk — I am the project manager of record for this product.\n\nIf the sponsor hears my concerns and decides to continue, I escalate to the next governance level. In a financial services organization this would typically be the ethics committee, the risk committee, or the general counsel, depending on the nature of the ethical concern.\n\nIf after escalation the organization decides to proceed, I make a personal decision about whether I can continue to manage the project. I am not obligated to remain on a project that conflicts with my professional values, and resigning from a specific project is a legitimate choice.`,
