  q: "How do you calculate schedule variance and cost variance in a project and what do they tell you?",
  a: `Schedule variance and cost variance are two of the core metrics in earned value management and they measure project performance in terms that are directly comparable to the approved baseline.\n\nSchedule variance is calculated as earned value minus planned value. Earned value is the budgeted cost of the work that has actually been completed. Planned value is the budgeted cost of the work that should have been completed by now according to the baseline schedule.\n\nIf earned value is 80 and planned value is 100, the schedule variance is negative 20. This means the project is behind schedule — you have completed 80 worth of work when you should have completed 100. If the schedule variance is positive, you are ahead of schedule.\n\nCost variance is calculated as earned value minus actual cost. Actual cost is what you have actually spent to date.\n\nIf earned value is 80 and actual cost is 90, the cost variance is negative 10. This means you have spent more than the budgeted cost for the work you have completed. You have spent 90 to produce 80 worth of work. If the cost variance is positive, you are under budget for the work completed.\n\nThese two metrics together give you a more complete picture than either alone. A project can be behind schedule and over budget, behind schedule and under budget, ahead of schedule and over budget, or ahead of schedule and under budget. Each combination suggests different things about what is happening on the project.\n\nIn financial services projects I use these metrics to produce a forecast at completion — an estimate of what the project will cost and when it will finish if current performance continues. This forecast, produced early enough, gives the project sponsor and steering committee time to make decisions rather than discovering the final position at project close.`,
