  q: "What is resource leveling and when should you use it?",
  a: `Resource leveling is a schedule optimization technique that adjusts task start and end dates to resolve resource conflicts — situations where the same resource is required by multiple tasks at the same time, creating a demand that exceeds their available capacity.\n\nIn most project schedules, tasks are initially scheduled based on logical dependencies and constraints without considering whether the resources assigned to those tasks are actually available when needed. The result is often a schedule that shows multiple tasks running in parallel, all assigned to the same person or team. The person cannot work on all of them simultaneously, so in practice some of the tasks will be delayed.\n\nResource leveling resolves these conflicts by pushing task start dates out until the resource is available, by changing task assignments so different resources handle tasks that were originally assigned to the same resource, or by extending task durations to reflect the part-time allocation of a resource that is shared across multiple tasks.\n\nThe trade-off with resource leveling is that it typically extends the project schedule. When you push tasks out to resolve resource conflicts, some of those tasks may be on the critical path and the project completion date moves accordingly. This is the honest cost of having a realistic schedule — one that can actually be executed with the resources available — rather than an optimistic one that assumes resources can be in two places at once.\n\nI use resource leveling when I am building the initial project schedule and when I reassess the schedule after significant changes to resource availability. In telecom infrastructure projects where the same field engineers are assigned to multiple parallel workstreams, resource leveling is essential to producing a schedule that reflects what can actually be executed. In agile projects, the sprint planning process achieves a similar effect by capping work to the team's available capacity for each sprint.`,
