        q: "You are managing a program that involves three parallel product teams working on interconnected features. One team hits a technical blocker that affects the other two. How do you unblock them?",
        subcategory: null,
        difficulty: 'Hard',
        domain: 'software_saas',
        a: `A blocker in one team that ripples across three teams is a program-level risk, not a project problem. The solution is not to order the blocked team to work faster. It is to redesign the dependency.

In past programs, I learned that the worst response is to escalate and demand. The best response is to make the problem visible to all three teams and co-create a solution.

I called an emergency sync with all three tech leads. I mapped the blocker at the technical level—they were blocked on a data schema that the first team had not finalized. I asked: if we deferred the schema decision and used a temporary interface instead, could team two and three continue forward? Yes. What are the risks? We carry technical debt that we will refactor in three weeks.

Then I made a trade-off proposal: delay the full integration by three weeks to resolve the schema cleanly, or proceed with a temporary interface and accept refactoring work. Team two and three said they could continue with the temporary interface and absorb the refactor. Team one said they needed two more weeks to unblock on the schema.

We chose the parallel path: teams two and three proceed with the temporary interface starting this week. Team one delivers the final schema in two weeks. Integration and refactor happens in week three. We compressed the end-to-end program timeline by one week.

The key was surfacing options, not just absorbing the blocker. Program management is about optimizing the whole, not just the part.`,
